  {
    "runtag": "cfda-chiq-llm4cs-splade-rrf",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "2-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How does brewing method change coffee taste compared to roasting?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of brewing on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The brewing method and roasting process both play crucial roles in determining the taste of coffee, but they affect it in different ways. \n\n**Roasting** influences the flavor by altering the chemical composition of the beans. Light roasts maintain more of the original bean flavors, resulting in fruity and floral notes with higher acidity, while dark roasts develop bolder, more bitter flavors due to longer roasting times, which also reduce acidity.\n\n**Brewing**, on the other hand, affects how these flavors are extracted from the coffee grounds. Different methods (like pour-over, French press, or espresso) extract flavors at different rates, impacting the balance of acidity, bitterness, and sweetness in the final cup. For example, a pour-over method tends to highlight bright, acidic flavors, while a French press may produce a fuller-bodied, thicker cup due to the longer steeping time.\n\nIn summary, roasting sets the foundational flavor profile of the coffee, while brewing method fine-tunes the extraction of those flavors, ultimately influencing the overall taste experience.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0044-36-03282:4": 1.5733489990234375,
            "clueweb22-en0022-00-18289:14": 1.411275863647461,
