        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, thanks! I\u2019ve tried a French press, but never cold brew\u2014does cold brew take a lot more time to make? And are there certain beans that are better for low-acid coffee?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Cold brew coffee typically takes 12-24 hours to steep, which is significantly longer than other brewing methods. However, it's a low-maintenance process that can result in a smooth, low-acid coffee. For low-acid coffee, look for beans that are naturally low in acidity, such as Arabica beans or those from regions like Brazil or Sumatra. You can also experiment with darker roasts, as they tend to have a lower acidity level than lighter roasts. This should help you find a coffee that suits your taste preferences and is gentle on your stomach.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super useful. Are there other major types of coffee beans besides Arabica? What makes each type different?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Besides Arabica, the other major type of coffee bean is Robusta. Robusta beans have a bolder and more bitter flavor compared to Arabica, with a higher caffeine content. They are often used in instant coffee and espresso blends. Arabica beans, on the other hand, have a milder and more nuanced flavor. There are also other, lesser-known types of coffee beans, such as Liberica and Excelsa, but Arabica and Robusta are the most widely available and consumed. The main difference between these types is the flavor profile and caffeine content, which can affect the overall taste and character of the coffee. As someone who prefers a smooth taste, Arabica might be a better fit for you.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! Can you compare the caffeine content and shape of Excelsa and Liberica coffee beans?",
