        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense. Switching gears a bit: what is the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The roasting process profoundly impacts coffee's taste. Lighter roasts retain more of the bean's natural acidity, resulting in brighter, fruitier, or floral notes. As roasting progresses to medium and dark, acidity generally decreases, contributing to a smoother, less acidic profile, which is gentler on the stomach. Darker roasts also develop more bitterness, often with notes of chocolate, roasted nuts, or earthiness, due to the breakdown of acids and the formation of new compounds like NMP. Sweetness is enhanced through caramelization and Maillard reactions, and lipids contribute to the coffee's body.",
