      },
      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "So, brewing makes a difference beyond roasting, right? How much does the brewing method affect the final coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of brewing on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Brewing methods drastically alter coffee taste, impacting strength, bitterness, and acidity. For stomach-friendly, non-bitter coffee, **cold brew** is excellent, being naturally low in acidity (up to 70% less) and mellow.\n\n**Chemex** and **filter coffee** are known for smooth results. A **French Press** can also yield a smooth, non-bitter cup with coarse grounds. Methods like **Turkish, Moka Pot, or Percolator** are prone to bitterness due to high temperatures or fine grinds. Crucially, grind size, water temperature, and brew time prevent over-extraction, which causes bitterness and can increase acidity.",
          "citations": {
