          "user_utterance": "I want to know how to make a really good cup of coffee. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here \u2019 s a simple, repeatable path to great coffee, with options gentle on the stomach. gear basics - fresh beans ( roasted within 2 \u2013 4 weeks ), burr grinder, scale, filtered / mineralized water. water - temp : 88 \u2013 92\u00b0c for smoother / less acidic ; 93 \u2013 96\u00b0c for brighter cups. - good minerals help flavor and reduce harshness. aim ~ 60 \u2013 100 ppm hardness with some alkalinity. pour - over ( v60 / chemex ) - ratio : 1 : 16 ( e. g., 22 g coffee to 350 g water ). - grind : medium ; target total brew 3 : 00 \u2013 4 : 00. - bloom : 2 \u2013 3x coffee weight, 30 \u2013 45 s, gentle swirl. - pour in 2 \u2013 3 pulses, keep the bed flat, minimal agitation. - taste tuning : sour / thin \u2192 finer grind / hotter / slower. bitter / dry \u2192 coarser / cooler / less agitation. immersion ( french press / aeropress ) - ratio : 1 : 14 \u2013 1 : 15 ( immersion needs a bit more coffee ). - french press : medium - coarse, 4 min steep, break crust, skim,",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-28-07734:15": -0.36789554357528687,
            "clueweb22-en0010-48-15668:15": -0.378566712141037,
            "clueweb22-en0009-38-13361:13": -0.38617509603500366,
            "clueweb22-en0014-82-01220:14": -0.4207225739955902,
            "clueweb22-en0020-29-15754:11": -0.4207225739955902
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super helpful, thanks! What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here are the main coffee bean types you \u2019 ll see : - arabica ( coffea arabica ) : ~ 60 \u2013 70 % of global coffee. sweeter, more aromatic and complex, with brighter acidity and lower caffeine. great for pour - over and drip. for gentler cups, look for low - acid arabicas ( e. g., brazil, sumatra ) or medium - dark roasts. - robusta ( coffea canephora ) : the other major commercial type. hardier plant with higher caffeine, stronger bitterness, earthy / nutty notes, heavy body, and big crema in espresso. can be harsher on the stomach ; often used in espresso blends. - liberica ( coffea liberica ) : under ~ 2 % of production. large beans ; bold, smoky / woody with tropical - fruit tones ; very aromatic. grows tall and is disease - resistant \u2014 fun if you like trying something new. - excelsa ( coffea liberica var. dewevrei ) : often listed as a separate type but now considered a variety of liberica. tart, fruity, light body ; commonly",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0031-65-00073:1": -0.5658385157585144,
            "clueweb22-en0035-25-05669:0": -0.6475847959518433,
            "clueweb22-en0001-20-10010:19": -0.6792541742324829,
            "clueweb22-en0031-65-00073:2": -0.9999094009399414,
            "clueweb22-en0035-25-05669:1": -1.11026132106781
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can you compare the caffeine content and shape of Excelsa and Liberica coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you compare the caffeine content and shape of Excelsa and Liberica coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "- Caffeine   - Excelsa: about 0.86\u20131.13% caffeine by weight (lower than arabica and much lower than robusta).   - Liberica: generally in the arabica-like range, roughly around 1\u20131.5% by weight, so typically higher than excelsa but far below robusta.   - If you\u2019re sensitive to caffeine or acidity, excelsa can be a gentler option.  - Bean shape   - Liberica: typically large, asymmetrical, and almond/tear\u2011drop shaped (often with a slight \u201chook\u201d on one end).   - Excelsa: frequently described as smaller and rounder than Liberica; some producers note it can look similar to Liberica\u2019s almond shape since it\u2019s a Liberica variety. In practice, size/shape can vary by source and cultivar.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0014-77-04149:9": -8.08802604675293,
            "clueweb22-en0004-97-11464:6": -8.28848648071289,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:4": -8.726255416870117,
            "clueweb22-en0032-30-02762:8": -8.76991081237793,
            "clueweb22-en0016-45-10462:1": -8.810478210449219
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! What about the taste and acidity between Excelsa and Liberica? Which one would be easier on my stomach?",
          "user_rubrik": "Which one of Liberica and Excelsa is better for me?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "taste - liberica : bold, full - bodied, very aromatic ; smoky / woody with floral or tropical - fruit aromas. can be earthy. some lots ( well processed ) taste lighter with subtle fruit / floral notes. - excelsa : lighter body but much fruitier, with a distinct tart, \u201c winey \u201d brightness and a hint of smokiness. often used to add complexity in blends. acidity - excelsa generally shows higher, tarter acidity. - liberica tends to have lower perceived acidity and a rounder profile. easier on the stomach? - if your reflux is triggered by acidity, a medium / medium - dark liberica is usually gentler. - if caffeine is the bigger trigger, excelsa has slightly lower caffeine and may sit better \u2014 choose a medium roast to avoid sharp tartness. brew tips for gentler cups - use 88 \u2013 92\u00b0c water, medium to medium - dark roast. - prefer immersion ( french press / aeropress ) or cold brew ; keep agitation low. - use filtered water with some alkalinity. - if you tolerate it, a small splash of milk can help buffer acidity ( watch calories",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-88-13848:5": -0.018028028309345245,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:4": -0.35304704308509827,
