      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "This is really helpful, thanks! Now, what\u2019s the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "roasting changes coffee through maillard reactions and caramelization, shifting flavor, body, acidity, and extraction behavior. - light roast : origin - forward ( fruit / floral ), higher perceived acidity, lighter body. denser beans extract slower \u2014 use slightly finer grind and hotter water. can feel brighter on the stomach. - medium roast : balanced origin + sweetness ( caramel, toffee, nutty / chocolate ), moderate body, reduced acidity. great daily driver and often gentler. - medium - dark : more chocolate / cocoa, caramelized sweetness, heavier body, lower acidity ; a smooth finish when well roasted. often easier on reflux. - dark roast : roast - driven ( smoky, roasty, sometimes bitter if overdone ), very low perceived acidity, oils on surface, high solubility. gentler on acidity but watch bitterness. other effects : - caffeine : differences by roast are small per cup ; darker roasts are less dense, so by scoop you may get slightly less caffeine. - grind / brew : darker roasts extract faster \u2014 go a touch coarser / cooler ; lighter roasts finer / hotter. - freshness : rest",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-74-04438:1": -3.0305140018463135,
            "clueweb22-en0035-74-04438:0": -3.2820425033569336,
            "clueweb22-en0007-65-09218:4": -3.7614877223968506,
            "clueweb22-en0024-16-15011:0": -4.25615119934082,
            "clueweb22-en0024-16-15011:1": -4.352346897125244
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great info! Does brewing method or time affect bitterness or sweetness in the cup?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of brewing on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes. Brewing method and contact time drive extraction, which sets acidity, sweetness, and bitterness.  - Sequence: acids extract first, then sweetness, then bitterness. Too short = sour/thin; too long = bitter/dry. - Time/grind/temp levers:   - More extraction (finer grind, longer time, hotter water, more agitation) \u2192 more sweetness then bitterness.   - Less extraction (coarser, shorter, cooler, gentle) \u2192 brighter/sour.  Method differences - Espresso (short, high pressure, fine grind): emphasizes acidity and sweetness with less bitterness when dialed in. - Drip/pour-over (longer, medium grind): more balanced; can tip bitter if flow is slow or grind too fine. - Immersion (French press/AeroPress): can be round and sweet at ~3\u20134 min with a coarse-to-medium grind; steeping too long or grinding too fine increases bitterness.  Quick tuning - Sour/thin \u2192 grind finer or brew a bit longer/hotter. - Bitter/dry \u2192 grind coarser or brew shorter/cooler, reduce agitation.  For a gentler cup on the stomach, aim for moderate extraction and 88\u201392\u00b0C water.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-16-13801:13": -0.5230458974838257,
            "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:4": -1.209725022315979,
            "clueweb22-en0035-10-05441:11": -1.2613651752471924,
            "clueweb22-en0035-54-19241:9": -1.6721878051757812,
            "clueweb22-en0022-45-17011:14": -1.7021310329437256
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
