  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "2-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about how to make good coffee. What are the most important things to start with?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Start with these core elements \u2014 they control most of the taste:  - Beans & freshness: use freshly roasted, freshly ground beans. Buy whole beans and grind just before brewing. - Grinder & grind consistency: use a burr grinder. Match grind size to method \u2014 coarse for cold brew/French press, medium for drip, fine for espresso. If coffee is bitter, try coarser; if sour/astringent, try finer. - Coffee-to-water ratio: weigh your coffee. Common starting points: drip ~1:16\u20131:18 (\u224860 g per liter), French press ~1:14\u20131:15, cold brew roughly 1 lb per gallon (or experiment 1:8\u20131:10 for concentrate). - Water temperature: for hot methods, heat to about 90\u201396\u00b0C (195\u2013205\u00b0F). For cold brew, use room-temp or cold water and long steeping. - Brew time and method: steeping/extraction time affects strength and flavor (cold brew 12\u201324 hours). Adjust time to taste. - Measure and record: use a scale, take notes, and adjust one variable at a time until you like the result.  Start with these, then tweak grind, ratio, time, and temperature to dial in your perfect cup.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-26-09295:17": 52.25469970703125,
            "clueweb22-en0034-07-03822:13": 51.64690017700195,
            "clueweb22-en0016-94-12627:7": 50.74140167236328,
            "clueweb22-en0012-92-14335:3": 50.61159896850586,
            "clueweb22-en0018-45-09231:0": 49.86970138549805
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, thanks! What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "There are four main commercial coffee species to know:  - Arabica (Coffea arabica): The most common. Grown at higher altitudes, slower-maturing\u2014often sweeter, more complex and aromatic. Base for most specialty coffee.  - Robusta (Coffea canephora): Hardier plant, grows at lower altitudes, higher caffeine. Tends toward a heavier body, more bitterness and dark\u2011chocolate/earthy notes; often used in espresso blends for crema and body.  - Liberica (Coffea liberica): Rare (small % of global production). Large, asymmetrical beans with a bold, earthy/smoky or nutty profile (e.g., Kapeng Barako). Popular regionally in Southeast Asia.  - Excelsa: Historically treated as a separate species but now usually classed as a variety of Liberica. Used mainly for blending to add complexity and tart/fruitier notes.  Note: roast and grind matter as much as bean species\u2014roast profile strongly shapes final flavor and best brewing method.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-07405:2": 52.27130126953125,
            "clueweb22-en0029-40-16358:5": 52.26100158691406,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:3": 48.920101165771484,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:2": 48.75339889526367,
            "clueweb22-en0045-33-05805:0": 48.66339874267578
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can you compare the caffeine content and shape of Excelsa and Liberica coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you compare the caffeine content and shape of Excelsa and Liberica coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer \u2014 they\u2019re similar but not identical.  Caffeine - Excelsa: generally a bit lower \u2014 roughly ~0.9\u20131.1 g per 100 g of green beans (commonly cited \u22481.0 g/100 g). - Liberica: slightly higher on average (around ~1.2\u20131.3 g per 100 g). Note: these are approximate ranges; variety, growing conditions and processing cause variation, and roast/grind/brew affect caffeine in your cup.  Shape and size - Liberica: larger, asymmetrical beans often described as almond\u2011shaped. - Excelsa: smaller, rounder beans compared with liberica. Taxonomy note: Excelsa is usually treated as a variety of Liberica now, but bean shape and flavor differences are still noticeable.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0004-97-11464:6": 39.55830001831055,
            "clueweb22-en0035-25-05669:12": 37.81760025024414,
            "clueweb22-en0004-97-11464:7": 37.24039840698242,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:4": 34.55400085449219,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:3": 34.38460159301758
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Which one of Liberica and Excelsa is better for me?",
          "user_rubrik": "Which one of Liberica and Excelsa is better for me?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "It depends on what you like.  - Pick Liberica if you prefer bold, earthy, smoky or nutty flavors and a heavier, more singular character. High\u2011quality Liberica can also show surprising floral or fruity notes, but overall it\u2019s the richer, more rustic option. It also tends to have slightly more caffeine (\u22481.2\u20131.3 g per 100 g green beans).  - Pick Excelsa if you want brighter, fruitier and tart notes with complexity and a hint of smokiness\u2014it's often used in blends to add lift and complexity. Excelsa generally has a bit less caffeine (around ~1.0 g/100 g) and a different, slightly smaller bean shape.  Best approach: buy small bags or sample flights (single\u2011origin or small roasts) of each, brew them the way you like, and compare. If you can\u2019t decide, try a blend that includes both to get balance.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0035-25-05669:12": 45.8577995300293,
            "clueweb22-en0035-25-05669:11": 45.806400299072266,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:3": 44.46329879760742,
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-07405:2": 41.446800231933594,
            "clueweb22-en0036-21-16351:4": 41.224098205566406
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's helpful! How does roasting affect coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Roasting transforms green beans into the flavors and aromas you taste. Key effects and why they happen:  - Chemical changes: Heat drives the Maillard reaction (browning) that creates many new aroma compounds (bready, toasty, caramel notes). At higher temperatures (pyrolysis) oils and volatile acids break down or form darker, roasty compounds. - Roast levels and typical taste:   - Light roast: preserves origin character\u2014bright, crisp acidity, floral/fruity and delicate sweetness; light body.   - Medium roast: mellows bright acids, develops caramel/honey and nut/chocolate notes; more balanced and rounded.   - Dark roast: origin notes smooth out; flavors shift to chocolate, molasses, spice, earth and heavier body; beans often show surface oil and more roast-driven bitterness. - Tradeoffs: darker roasts show less of the bean\u2019s regional character; very dark roasts can \u201croast away\u201d pleasant complexity. Caffeine content is broadly similar across roast levels. - Practical: choose roast to match desired flavor (origin clarity vs. roasty richness). Grind and brew method then extract those developed aroma compounds.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0041-11-10311:3": 66.6448974609375,
            "clueweb22-en0022-30-10158:19": 66.6343002319336,
            "clueweb22-en0022-30-10158:18": 66.12480163574219,
            "clueweb22-en0025-26-13258:5": 64.62529754638672,
            "clueweb22-en0039-27-01568:4": 64.3988037109375
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How does brewing impact the flavor notes that come from roasting?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of brewing on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "brewing choices either reveal or mask the flavors developed in roasting. key effects and short tips : - water temperature : hotter water extracts more solubles and tends to mute brightness while emphasizing smooth, roasty, bitter or chocolatey notes ; cooler water preserves acidity and origin brightness. adjust a few degrees to favor roast or origin character. - contact time / method : short contact ( espresso, fast pour ) favors roast \u2011 driven, caramel / roast notes ; long contact ( french press, cold brew ) pulls out more body, richness and heavier roast flavors ( or mellows acidity ). - grind size / flow resistance : finer grind = faster, stronger extraction ( more bitterness and body ) ; coarser = cleaner, brighter cup. match grind to method to avoid over / under \u2011 extraction. - ratio and agitation : stronger ratios and more agitation amplify all flavors ( including roastiness ) ; weaker ratios soften them. practical moves - to highlight roast ( chocolate, smoke, bitters ) : slightly coarser grind, shorter brew time or quicker pour, a touch hotter water. - to highlight roast \u2019 s subtlety or origin notes : slightly cooler water, finer grind ( if needed ), slower pour / longer",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0028-67-10382:16": 65.893798828125,
            "clueweb22-en0013-59-03217:3": 62.295799255371094,
            "clueweb22-en0012-66-03651:4": 61.49789810180664,
            "clueweb22-en0017-09-06917:10": 57.54859924316406,
            "clueweb22-en0028-40-03281:7": 57.40119934082031
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
