      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about how to make good coffee. What are the most important things to start with?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Start with these core elements \u2014 they control most of the taste:  - Beans & freshness: use freshly roasted, freshly ground beans. Buy whole beans and grind just before brewing. - Grinder & grind consistency: use a burr grinder. Match grind size to method \u2014 coarse for cold brew/French press, medium for drip, fine for espresso. If coffee is bitter, try coarser; if sour/astringent, try finer. - Coffee-to-water ratio: weigh your coffee. Common starting points: drip ~1:16\u20131:18 (\u224860 g per liter), French press ~1:14\u20131:15, cold brew roughly 1 lb per gallon (or experiment 1:8\u20131:10 for concentrate). - Water temperature: for hot methods, heat to about 90\u201396\u00b0C (195\u2013205\u00b0F). For cold brew, use room-temp or cold water and long steeping. - Brew time and method: steeping/extraction time affects strength and flavor (cold brew 12\u201324 hours). Adjust time to taste. - Measure and record: use a scale, take notes, and adjust one variable at a time until you like the result.  Start with these, then tweak grind, ratio, time, and temperature to dial in your perfect cup.",
