  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "2-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm curious about making good coffee! What's the most important thing to start with?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Start with the beans \u2014 they matter more than gadgets. Buy fresh, whole\u2011bean coffee from a roaster you trust and pick a low\u2011acid or medium\u2011to\u2011dark roast if you get mild reflux (darker roasts and beans labeled \u201clow\u2011acid\u201d tend to be gentler). Grind just before brewing for best flavor.  Practical next steps: - Grind size matched to your method (coarse for French press, medium for drip, fine for espresso). - Water: filtered, about 195\u2013205\u00b0F; use a 1:15\u20131:17 coffee:water ratio to start and tweak. - Try cold brew or a gentler brew method (slow pour-over or French press)\u2014cold brew is reliably lower in acid. - Don\u2019t drink your coffee on an empty stomach; have something small first and sip slowly. - Store beans airtight, cool, away from light; buy small amounts to keep them fresh.  If you want, tell me your brewing gear and I\u2019ll give a specific recipe.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0006-38-17827:0": -6.077678680419922,
            "clueweb22-en0040-56-13241:0": -6.666187286376953,
            "clueweb22-en0017-34-08544:3": -6.755482196807861,
            "clueweb22-en0005-60-11657:6": -6.796412944793701,
            "clueweb22-en0044-51-00595:5": -6.947942733764648
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! That\u2019s helpful. What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Quick overview \u2014 four commonly referenced coffee species:  - Arabica (Coffea arabica): Most specialty coffee. Sweeter, more complex flavors (fruity, floral, chocolatey), generally higher acidity. Grown at higher altitudes; best for single\u2011origin and nuanced cups.  - Robusta (Coffea canephora): Hardier, higher yield. Stronger, more bitter/grainy profile with good crema; generally less acidic than Arabica and often used in blends, instant coffee, and some espresso blends. Higher caffeine content.  - Liberica: Rarer and less common worldwide. Produces distinct, bold, sometimes woody or floral flavors \u2014 not as widely available but used regionally.  - Excelsa: Once classed separately, now often treated near Liberica; has unique tart/fruitier notes and can be specialty\u2011grade when processed/roasted well.  Blends combine species to balance body, crema, acidity and price. For mild reflux, consider darker roasts or beans labeled \u201clow\u2011acid\u201d regardless of species.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-87-19028:2": -2.0306971073150635,
