    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm curious about making good coffee! What's the most important thing to start with?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Start with the beans \u2014 they matter more than gadgets. Buy fresh, whole\u2011bean coffee from a roaster you trust and pick a low\u2011acid or medium\u2011to\u2011dark roast if you get mild reflux (darker roasts and beans labeled \u201clow\u2011acid\u201d tend to be gentler). Grind just before brewing for best flavor.  Practical next steps: - Grind size matched to your method (coarse for French press, medium for drip, fine for espresso). - Water: filtered, about 195\u2013205\u00b0F; use a 1:15\u20131:17 coffee:water ratio to start and tweak. - Try cold brew or a gentler brew method (slow pour-over or French press)\u2014cold brew is reliably lower in acid. - Don\u2019t drink your coffee on an empty stomach; have something small first and sip slowly. - Store beans airtight, cool, away from light; buy small amounts to keep them fresh.  If you want, tell me your brewing gear and I\u2019ll give a specific recipe.",
