    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! That\u2019s helpful. What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Quick overview \u2014 four commonly referenced coffee species:  - Arabica (Coffea arabica): Most specialty coffee. Sweeter, more complex flavors (fruity, floral, chocolatey), generally higher acidity. Grown at higher altitudes; best for single\u2011origin and nuanced cups.  - Robusta (Coffea canephora): Hardier, higher yield. Stronger, more bitter/grainy profile with good crema; generally less acidic than Arabica and often used in blends, instant coffee, and some espresso blends. Higher caffeine content.  - Liberica: Rarer and less common worldwide. Produces distinct, bold, sometimes woody or floral flavors \u2014 not as widely available but used regionally.  - Excelsa: Once classed separately, now often treated near Liberica; has unique tart/fruitier notes and can be specialty\u2011grade when processed/roasted well.  Blends combine species to balance body, crema, acidity and price. For mild reflux, consider darker roasts or beans labeled \u201clow\u2011acid\u201d regardless of species.",
          "citations": {
