          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about making good coffee. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to make a good coffee?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Nice \u2014 since you start your day with coffee and prefer gentle beverages, try these practical tips:  - Use freshly roasted whole beans and a burr grinder; grind just before brewing.   - Match grind to method: coarse for French press, medium for drip, fine for espresso.   - Weigh coffee: try 1:15\u20131:17 coffee:water ratio (e.g., 20 g coffee \u2192 300 g water).   - Brew temp: 90\u201396\u00b0C normally; lower (\u224885\u201392\u00b0C) or cold brew (12\u201324 hrs) to reduce acidity.   - Choose dark-roast or beans labeled \u201clow-acid\u201d and use a paper filter to remove oils/acids.   - Use filtered water, rinse paper filters, and keep gear clean for consistent flavor.   - Skip sugar (you already do); add a dash of cinnamon or a small splash of milk/plant milk if reflux is an issue.   - Experiment: try Aeropress or pour-over for control. Weigh, note changes, and iterate \u2014 fits your tinkerer/dev mindset.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Sounds awesome, thanks! What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the different types of coffee beans?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "broadly there are four coffee species you \u2019 ll see most often : - arabica ( coffea arabica ) : most common. cleaner, more complex, higher acidity and nuanced flavors ( fruit, floral, chocolate ). varieties include typica, bourbon, caturra, sl28 / sl34, geisha / gesha. - robusta ( coffea canephora ) : higher caffeine, more bitter and earthy, thicker crema \u2014 often used in espresso blends and instant coffee. - liberica : rare ; woody, smoky, sometimes floral and fruity \u2014 distinctive and uneven availability. - excelsa ( now classed with liberica ) : tart, fruity, often used to add complexity to blends. other useful distinctions : - origin matters : brazilian, sumatran, and some indian / monsooned coffees tend to be lower - acid and fuller - bodied \u2014 good if you \u2019 re reflux - prone. - processing : washed ( cleaner, brighter acid ) ; natural / dry ( fruitier, heavier body ). - roast : darker roasts reduce perceived acidity and highlight cocoa / roasted notes \u2014 fits your taste and digestion needs. try",
