          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of roasting on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Roasting transforms green beans chemically and physically, and it\u2019s the single biggest factor after origin that shapes taste.  Key effects - Light roast: brighter, higher acidity, floral/fruity/tea\u2011like origin notes are clear. More perceived acidity. - Medium roast: balanced acidity and body, sweeter caramel/ nutty flavors; origin character still present. - Medium\u2011dark: deeper caramelization, chocolate/toast notes, less acidity, fuller body. - Dark roast: smoky, bitter, roasted/cocoa flavors, oily surface, lowest perceived acidity; origin flavors largely masked.  Why: Maillard reactions, caramelization and pyrolysis create new aroma compounds and reduce acids; longer/hotter roasting builds roast-derived flavors and slightly reduces caffeine. Heavier roasts also increase body and oil on the bean, affecting extraction.  Practical for you: choose darker roasts or \u201clow\u2011acid\u201d labels, use paper filters or cold brew and lower brew temp to reduce reflux risk while keeping a pleasing, fuller cup.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How does brewing method change the taste of coffee?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is the impact of brewing on coffee taste?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "brewing method changes taste by altering extraction ( what compounds dissolve ), contact time, temperature, pressure, and filtration \u2014 all of which affect acidity, sweetness, body, clarity and bitterness. quick breakdown : - pour \u2011 over ( v60, kalita ) : single \u2011 pass pour, paper filter \u2192 clean, bright, nuanced ; highlights origin acidity. - drip : similar to pour \u2011 over but less manual control ; clean, balanced. - french press : full immersion + metal filter \u2192 heavy body, more oils / sediment, rounder but can be more bitter. - espresso : high pressure, short time \u2192 concentrated, intense, crema, pronounced roast / bitterness. - aeropress : highly versatile ; can mimic immersion or pour \u2011 over ; paper filter gives clarity, metal disk gives more body. - cold brew : long, low \u2011 temp steep \u2192 very smooth, low acidity, sweeter \u2014 good for reflux. - moka pot : stovetop pressure \u2192 strong, sometimes harsh / over \u2011 extracted. - siphon : clean, aromatic, balanced. for your reflux : prefer dark roast, paper filters or cold brew, slightly lower brew temp, and coarser grinds for",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
